# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
/node_modules
/.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# testing
/coverage

# TanStack Start's build output. This is what `npm run build` writes and the
# Dockerfile copies, so it is an artifact and never source.
#
# The route tree is the deliberate exception — `src/routeTree.gen.ts` IS
# committed, because CI typechecks before it builds and `tsc --noEmit` has to
# resolve the router's import on a clean checkout. That was true under the
# previous framework too (it committed `next-env.d.ts` for the same reason) and
# it is the one piece of generated source in the tree. See `src/router.tsx`.
/dist/
/.tanstack/
# Nitro's build output, from `npm run build:node` — the Node/Docker target's
# equivalent of `dist/`. Same reason, same rule: it is an artifact.
#
# wrangler's scratch space: the plugin's generated per-build state and anything
# `wrangler dev` / `deploy --dry-run` leaves behind. `wrangler.jsonc` IS
# committed for the same reason `cafe.dev.json` is — it is the configuration the
# deploy reads, not a build artifact. `wrangler.jsonc`'s own comments say which
# of the three fields earn their place.
/.output/
/.wrangler/

# production
/build

# misc
.DS_Store
*.pem

# debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# env files (can opt-in for committing if needed)
.env*
# …and the opt-in, named, because `.env*` above matches `.env.example` too and a
# `git add .env.example` on a file git considers ignored says NOTHING — no
# error, no hint — while the clone has no example and the commit looks like it
# worked. `.env.example` is the file a contributor copies to `.env`; the local
# `.env` it produces stays ignored, which is the whole of what this line buys.
!.env.example

# vercel
.vercel

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo

# test + tooling output
# (/coverage already listed above)
/test-results

# Playwright. `e2e-results.json` and the output directory are this run's
# artifacts, not source: they are written by `bin/e2e` and uploaded by CI, and
# the one that must never be committed is the record of session tokens (which
# lives in the OS temp directory, not here — see e2e/session-tokens.ts).
e2e-results.json
e2e/.artifacts/
playwright-report/

# kit's lint configs, fetched into the tree by kit's shared `ci` job
# (`KIT_LINT_DIR` is `<working-dir>/.kit`) and by the same checkout step a
# developer makes to reproduce a lint finding locally. It is CI's scratch
# space, not source: kit says so itself ("It is not copied into a service
# repo"), and committing a copy would reinstate exactly the mechanism W15
# ended. kit's own eslint config ignores `.kit/**` so the linted tree never
# contains it either.
/.kit/

# caf dev's rendered stack. `cafe.dev.json` IS committed — it is the catalog the
# local stack is built from, and a catalog nobody commits is a catalog that only
# exists on the machine that wrote it. This file is not: `caf dev` prints "Do
# not edit: every run rewrites this file", so committing it would be committing
# a generated artifact that is a merge conflict waiting to happen.
cafe.dev.compose.yaml
keyring/
secrets/
